---
// src/components/blog/visuals/SensoryProcessingVisual.astro
---

<div class="apple-glass-card apple-specular-top my-10 p-5 sm:p-7 rounded-3xl border border-gray-100 shadow-sm bg-gradient-to-br from-white/95 via-gray-50/50 to-emerald-50/20 text-left">
  <div class="flex items-center gap-2.5 mb-4">
    <span class="w-2.5 h-2.5 rounded-full bg-emerald-600"></span>
    <h3 class="font-bold text-brand-navy text-lg sm:text-xl tracking-tight m-0">
      The Sensory Cup Framework: Seeking vs. Avoiding Profiles
    </h3>
  </div>

  <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-gray-600 mb-6 leading-relaxed">
    Every child has a unique sensory threshold (Winnie Dunn's Sensory Processing Model). Knowing whether your child's "cup" is large or small explains why they bounce off walls or cover their ears.
  </p>

  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
    <!-- Sensory Seeking Card -->
    <div class="bg-white/90 border border-emerald-200/80 rounded-2xl p-5 shadow-2xs flex flex-col">
      <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-3">
        <span class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-emerald-900 bg-emerald-100 px-2.5 py-1 rounded-full">
          High Threshold &bull; Sensory Seeker
        </span>
        <span class="text-xs text-emerald-700 font-semibold">The "Giant Cup"</span>
      </div>

      <h4 class="font-bold text-brand-navy text-base mb-2">Cravings for Proprioceptive &amp; Vestibular Input</h4>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Their sensory cup is huge and constantly leaking. They need intense, heavy-work input just to feel grounded and register where their body is in space.
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-1.5 text-xs text-gray-700 mt-auto pt-3 border-t border-emerald-100">
        <p><strong>What it looks like:</strong> Crashing into sofas, spinning, chewing collars, intense body hugs, inability to sit still.</p>
        <p class="text-emerald-800"><strong>Clinical Antidote:</strong> Heavy work before seated tasks (wall pushes, carrying grocery bags, trampolines, bear crawls).</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Sensory Sensitive Card -->
    <div class="bg-white/90 border border-purple-200/80 rounded-2xl p-5 shadow-2xs flex flex-col">
      <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-3">
        <span class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-purple-900 bg-purple-100 px-2.5 py-1 rounded-full">
          Low Threshold &bull; Sensory Sensitive
        </span>
        <span class="text-xs text-purple-700 font-semibold">The "Tiny Cup"</span>
      </div>

      <h4 class="font-bold text-brand-navy text-base mb-2">Rapid Neurological Overload &amp; Shielding</h4>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Their cup is tiny and easily spills over. Modest ambient sounds, fluorescent lights, or scratchy clothing tags trigger an emergency pain alarm.
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-1.5 text-xs text-gray-700 mt-auto pt-3 border-t border-purple-100">
        <p><strong>What it looks like:</strong> Hands over ears, meltdowns at shopping malls, selective eating, screaming when hair is brushed.</p>
        <p class="text-purple-800"><strong>Clinical Antidote:</strong> Noise-dampening ear defenders, seamless socks, low-lighting calm corners, sensory retreat zones.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-5 p-3.5 bg-emerald-50/80 rounded-xl border border-emerald-200/50 text-xs text-gray-700 flex items-start gap-2">
    <span class="text-emerald-700 font-bold shrink-0">⚖️</span>
    <span><strong>Notice:</strong> Many ADHD and autistic children are <em>both</em>—they aggressively seek vestibular movement while simultaneously being hypersensitive to auditory or tactile input.</span>
  </div>
</div>
